<LandingLayout seo={seo}>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Whiteboard for Brainstorming: Techniques & Free Tools</h1>
  
  <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8">Brainstorming on a whiteboard is one of the most natural ways to generate ideas. Digital whiteboards bring this experience online, enabling remote teams to brainstorm together with the same energy and creativity as in-person sessions.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-4">Why Whiteboards Work for Brainstorming</h2>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Visual thinking activates different parts of the brain than linear note-taking. When you brainstorm on a whiteboard:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
    <li>Ideas become tangible — you can see, move, and group them</li>
    <li>Spatial relationships reveal connections between concepts</li>
    <li>The informal medium lowers the barrier to sharing half-formed thoughts</li>
    <li>Everyone can contribute simultaneously without waiting for turns</li>
    <li>The visual record persists after the session for reference</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-4">Proven Brainstorming Techniques</h2>
  
  <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">1. Classic Brainstorm (Diverge → Converge)</h3>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Set a timer for 10 minutes. Everyone adds ideas freely — no judgment, no discussion. Then group similar ideas, vote on favorites, and select the top 3 to develop further.</p>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">2. Mind Mapping</h3>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Start with a central question or problem. Branch out with related concepts, then branch further with specific ideas. The hierarchical structure helps explore a topic systematically.</p>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">3. SCAMPER Method</h3>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Apply seven thinking lenses to an existing idea: Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to other uses, Eliminate, Reverse. Create a section on the whiteboard for each lens.</p>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">4. Six Thinking Hats</h3>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Divide the whiteboard into six colored zones. Each zone represents a thinking mode: facts (white), emotions (red), caution (black), optimism (yellow), creativity (green), process (blue).</p>

  <h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-gray-800 mt-6 mb-3">5. Crazy Eights</h3>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Each participant gets 8 minutes to sketch 8 different solutions. Then share all sketches on the whiteboard and discuss the most promising approaches.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-4">Running Effective Remote Brainstorms</h2>
  <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-3 text-gray-700 mb-6">
    <li><strong>Set clear objectives</strong> — Define the problem statement before the session. Share it in advance so people come prepared.</li>
    <li><strong>Time-box everything</strong> — 5 minutes for individual ideation, 10 minutes for sharing, 5 minutes for voting. Constraints boost creativity.</li>
    <li><strong>Silent before social</strong> — Start with individual brainstorming before group discussion. This prevents groupthink and ensures introverts contribute.</li>
    <li><strong>Use visual prompts</strong> — Pre-populate the whiteboard with categories, questions, or inspiration images to jumpstart thinking.</li>
    <li><strong>Assign a facilitator</strong> — One person manages the timer, guides transitions, and ensures everyone participates.</li>
    <li><strong>Document outcomes</strong> — End with clear next steps: who does what by when. Export the whiteboard as the session record.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-4">Whiteboard Setup for Brainstorming</h2>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Before your session, prepare the canvas:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-700 mb-6">
    <li>Create labeled zones for different activities or categories</li>
    <li>Add the problem statement prominently at the top</li>
    <li>Include a parking lot area for off-topic but valuable ideas</li>
    <li>Set up a voting area for the convergence phase</li>
    <li>Add timer reminders or phase labels to guide the flow</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mt-8 mb-4">Why Excalidraw Hub for Brainstorming</h2>
  <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Excalidraw Hub's hand-drawn aesthetic removes the pressure of perfection. When everything looks like a sketch, people feel free to share rough ideas without self-censoring. The infinite canvas means you never run out of space, and real-time collaboration lets everyone contribute simultaneously.</p>

